\details{
When the polynomial degrees are too high, the curves can go out of control at both ends of the age range.
After fitting, check whether the mean same-age z of each wave is close to 0.

\code{cnorm.shash()} has several local solutions.
With the default start value, it sometimes stops at a broken solution.
There the tail parameter delta becomes very large, and the whole distribution is squeezed into a narrow band.
(KIT's cognition, all children: bad solution delta up to 52, -log-likelihood 184,112;
good solution delta 0.73-0.93, -log-likelihood 179,571.)
So one fit is run for each start value in \code{delta_starts}, and the solution with the largest likelihood is kept.
Only the cNORM warnings of the kept solution are shown.
Warnings from start values that are not kept are not shown.
When the kept solution's delta still exceeds \code{delta_max}, a warning is shown.

The age range can be computed with \code{\link[=kits_norm_ages]{kits_norm_ages()}}; then use \code{pad = 0}.
Screen the waves first with \code{\link[=kits_norm_screen]{kits_norm_screen()}}.
With \code{pad = 0}, data outside the range are not used, so you need not filter them yourself.

The first argument chooses the form (as in kits):
\itemize{
\item scores (numbers): \code{kits_norms(d, age_m, ages, ...)};
\item the scores of \code{\link[=kits_subj]{kits_subj()}} (with \code{age_m} from its \code{persons}): \code{kits_norms(s, ages, ...)} uses
their \code{theta} (convert it to D first, \code{s$theta <- d_link$intercept + d_link$slope * s$theta}) and \code{age_m}.
Then \code{ages} can be left out: the rule of \code{\link[=kits_norm_ages]{kits_norm_ages()}} sets them (from the 1st percentile
of age in the first wave to the 99th in the last), with \code{pad = 0} unless \code{pad} is given.
}

