scores of several domains are stacked with a \code{domain} column.
Each group gets exactly the norm that \code{kits_norms()} gives on the children of that group alone
(the same settings), and the result is one table with the group columns in front.
With scores of \code{\link[=kits_subj]{kits_subj()}}, \code{by} names their columns (\code{by = "sex"}, \code{by = c("domain", "sex")});
with scores as numbers, \code{by} is a data.frame of the group columns, one row per score.
\code{\link[=kits_norm_z]{kits_norm_z()}} then puts each child on the curves of its group, and \code{plot()} draws one panel per
